  "query_preview": "/Friday through Tuesday/... \nStrengthening of the ridge is forecast through Friday before \ngradually weakening this weekend into Monday. Guidance agrees H5 \nheights will rise to 596-598dm on Friday before dropping back to 594-\n596dm by Sunday. The strength of this ridge will easily be around \nthe 99th percentile of climatology across the region on Friday, \ndropping back to 90-97th percentile on Sunday. Due to the strength \nof the ridge and some slight drying, temperatures are forecast to \npeak on Friday and/or Saturday with highs 110-115 degrees. Surface \ndew points should fall back into the upper 50s to lower 60s during \nthis time, but it will still feel somewhat humid as heat indices \ncould reach as high as 115-120 degrees across the lower deserts of \nsoutheast California. There was enough confidence in guidance to \nalso extend the Extreme Heat Warning through Sunday as the \nconvective potential should be fairly limited. The strength of the \nhigh and slight dip in moisture is expected keep CIN values quite \nhigh across the area, especially over the lower deserts. Higher \nterrain areas of eastern Arizona will keep daily 15-30% chance PoPs, \nwhile the Phoenix area mostly stay between 10-15%. \n\nBy early next week, models show the ridge weakening again, while \nmost likely staying centered to our north. This should allow for \nbetter monsoon storm chances with models leaning toward some \nimproving moisture and better upper level support. NBM/WPC PoPs seem \ntoo high for Sunday at 30-40% over central and eastern Arizona, but \nthose chances may be more reasonable for Monday and/or Tuesday of \nnext week. Temperatures will also drift lower early next week due to \nthe weakening of the ridge and with more convective activity in the \narea. The latest NBM shows temperatures back into the normal range \nby around Monday or Tuesday.",
